der_colors":false,"scale":1},{"node_id":59788953,"draft_id":59788953,"ud_name":"","gen_name":"[object Object]","notes":"","draft_visible":true,"loom":null,"loom_settings":{"frames":8,"treadles":8,"epi":12,"ppi":12,"units":"in","type":"jacquard"},"render_colors":false,"scale":1},{"node_id":39391283,"draft_id":39391283,"ud_name":"","gen_name":"[object Object]","notes":"","draft_visible":true,"loom":null,"loom_settings":{"frames":8,"treadles":8,"epi":12,"ppi":12,"units":"in","type":"jacquard"},"render_colors":false,"scale":1},{"node_id":86930998,"draft_id":86930998,"ud_name":"","gen_name":"[object Object]","notes":"","draft_visible":true,"loom":null,"loom_settings":{"frames":8,"treadles":8,"epi":12,"ppi":12,"units":"in","type":"jacquard"},"render_colors":false,"scale":1},{"node_id":56978191,"draft_id":56978191,"ud_name":"","gen_name":"[object Object]","notes":"","draft_visible":true,"loom":null,"loom_settings":{"frames":8,"treadles":8,"epi":12,"ppi":12,"units":"in","type":"jacquard"},"render_colors":false,"scale":1}],"ops":[{"node_id":14609138,"name":"notation","params":["(a1)(d2)(c3)(b4)"],"inlets":[0,"a1","d2","c3","b4"]},{"node_id":44934534,"name":"tabby","params":[1,1,1,1],"inlets":[]},{"node_id":55454055,"name":"notation","params":["(a1)(c2)(d3)(b4)"],"inlets":[0,"a1","c2","d3","b4"]},{"node_id":88858515,"name":"notation","params":["(c1)(a2)(b3)(d4)"],"inlets":[0,"c1","a2","b3","d4"]},{"node_id":59310053,"name":"sample_width","params":["a64 b64 c64 d64 e64 f64 g64 h64"],"inlets":[0,"a","b","c","d","e","f","g","h"]},{"node_id":19937348,"name":"splice_in_wefts","params":[8,1,0],"inlets":[{"name":"receiving draft","type":"static","value":null,"uses":"draft","dx":"all the drafts you would like to interlace","num_drafts":1},{"name":"splicing draft","type":"static","value":null,"uses":"draft","dx":"the draft you would like to splice into the recieving draft","num_drafts":1}]},{"node_id":53182409,"name":"rectangle","params":[2640,144],"inlets":[0]},{"node_id":60234292,"name":"notation","params":["(c1)(b2)(a3)(d4)"],"inlets":[0,"c1","b2","a3","d4"]},{"node_id":99268495,"name":"notation","params":["(b1)(c2)(d3)(a4)"],"inlets":[0,"b1","c2","d3","a4"]},{"node_id":95379494,"name":"notation","params":["(b1)(d2)(c3)(a4)"],"inlets":[0,"b1","d2","c3","a4"]},{"node_id":62921656,"name":"notation","params":["(d1)(b2)(a3)(c4)"],"inlets":[0,"d1","b2","a3","c4"]},{"node_id":28516587,"name":"notation","params":["(d1)(a2)(b3)(c4)"],"inlets":[0,"d1","a2","b3","c4"]}],"notes":[{"id":94580613,"title":"1. Design Parts of the Lattice Structure","text":"As we show on the left, this design needs to have 8 different structural regions, one for each layer order present. \n\nWe use one LayerNotation Operation for each of these regions. This allows us to assign a weft and warp system a layer and to reorder those layers from top to bottom in the layer stack. Each parenthesis group is associated as a layer. \n\nThe inputs to layer notation include a systems draft and a structure for each layer. The systems draft represents the assignment of weft and warp systems to use when applying the notation. The layer notions creates an inlet for each layer, where the user can drag the draft they want to use on that layer. Here, we'll just use tabby for all layers. ","color":"#FFFF00","topleft":{"x":2185,"y":875},"imageurl":null,"width":353.8235294117648,"height":-296.76470588235287},{"id":83371601,"title":"2. Join Structures to Make a Repeating Unit","text":"I join all 8 structures in equal sizes. I created each segment to have 64 ends, which is corresponds to about 1\" on my loom. Each base structure repeats over 16 (as we can see from the size after the layer notation operation). So, I chose 64 so that it would be an even multiple. \n\nBecause we assigned each weft a different colored material, you can get a sense for which color will be on top in each region based on the color that is most dominant. ","color":"#FFFF00","topleft":{"x":2210,"y":2070},"imageurl":null,"width":554.9019607843138,"height":-465.9803921568627},{"id":56757503,"title":"3. Design the Floating Elastic Structure","text":"Now I'm going to design the structure that represents a float of elastic that travels under layers 1+2 and over layers 3+4 by creating a single pic with corresponding systems 1 and 2 lifted and 3 and 4 lowered. ","color":"#FFFF00","topleft":{"x":4644.067796610169,"y":3313.5593220338983},"imageurl":null,"width":551.8627450980392,"height":-1328.921568627451},{"id":86231005,"title":"4. Splice in the Elastic Floast","text":"I slice in the pics representing the elastic floats every 8 pics. Because there are 4 layers, it takes 4 pics to create one layer-pic. Beause I didn't want the elastic to be too bulky, I added it every other layer-pic, integrating it every 8 pics. Depending on how strong or weak it is, I can make the elastic stronger by adding it more frequently (at max, every 4 pics) or less frequently (12, 16, 20 pics, etc).","color":"#FFFF00","topleft":{"x":1706.214689265537,"y":3779.661016949153},"imageurl":null,"width":-1303.9473684210527,"height":-914.4736842105264},{"id":29756371,"title":"Lets Remake this Lattice Structure","text":"Designed by Elizabeth Meiklejohn in the 2023 Experimental Weaving Residency. \n\nSample 7b - EWR 2023\n4-layer lattice with elastic floats, 0 offset\nTechniques: multi-layer, shrinking floats\nWeft 1 (w1): 200 tex bonded nylon\nWeft 2 (w2): black elastic\nBase: plain weave in w1, 15 epi per layer\nFloats: w2 running through the center of the layer stack, not interlacing with anything. \n\nIn this file - we use different colors to note each weft, making it easier to visualize their movements\n\nView the full explaination at: https://docs.adacad.org/docs/learn/examples/lattice-tutorial/","color":"#FFFF00","topleft":{"x":231.63841807909606,"y":474.5762711864407},"imageurl":"https://docs.adacad.org/assets/images/lattice_open-e5a542ebfd37c63fed48bb048aefa301.jpg","width":893.5185185185184,"height":891.6666666666665},{"id":21535293,"title":"5. Fill Cloth","text":"Fill a rectangle representing the number of ends on my loom. I make the height equal to the repeat size of the draft above, which is 144. I'll weave this on a TC2 and set the structure to \"LOOP\" so I can weave as long as I like. ","color":"#FFFF00","topleft":{"x":1774.0112994350284,"y":5322.033898305085},"imageurl":null,"width":-752.0588235294117,"height":-2893.4313725490197}],"materials":[{"id":0,"name":"elastic ","stretch":1,"insert":true,"visible":true,"color":"#333333","thickness":100,"diameter":5,"type":0,"notes":"","rgb":{"r":51,"g":51,"b":51}},{"id":1,"name":"60/2 cotton warp","stretch":1,"insert":true,"visible":true,"color":"#ffffff","thickness":100,"diameter":5,"type":0,"notes":"","rgb":{"r":255,"g":255,"b":255}},{"id":2,"name":"200 tex bonded nylon","stretch":1,"insert":true,"visible":true,"color":"#c7f3ff","thickness":100,"diameter":5,"type":"0","notes":"","rgb":{"r":199,"g":243,"b":255}},{"id":3,"name":"Red Layer","stretch":1,"insert":true,"visible":true,"color":"#ff3030","thickness":100,"diameter":5,"type":0,"notes":"","rgb":{"r":255,"g":48,"b":48}},{"id":4,"name":"Green Layer","stretch":1,"insert":true,"visible":true,"color":"#c5ee19","thickness":100,"diameter":5,"type":0,"notes":"","rgb":{"r":197,"g":238,"b":25}},{"id":5,"name":"Blue Layer","stretch":1,"insert":true,"visible":true,"color":"#0b1278","thickness":100,"diameter":5,"type":0,"notes":"","rgb":{"r":11,"g":18,"b":120}},{"id":6,"name":"Cyan Layer","stretch":1,"insert":true,"visible":true,"color":"#73f8eb","thickness":100,"diameter":5,"type":0,"notes":"","rgb":{"r":115,"g":248,"b":235}}],"indexed_image_data":[]}